<section #sec:lists | Lists>

<sub-section | General Usage>

A list opens with `<list>` and closes with `</list>`. Each item is marked, and its content runs after the marker until the next item, a nested list, or `</list>` — no pipe, no closing bracket, the same way a section carries its body. An item mark comes in three registers:

Canonical: `<li>`
Sigil: `<->` or `<*>`
Markdown: `-` or `*`

<code #code:list-basic>
<list>
<li> First item
<li> Second item, which can run
across several lines and even hold
more than one paragraph.
</list>
</code>

<minipage #mp:list-basic>
<list>
<li> First item
<li> Second item, which can run
across several lines and even hold
more than one paragraph.
</list>
</minipage>

A list is numbered with `<list ordered>`, and nests by putting a `<list>` inside an item:

<code #code:list-ordered>
<list ordered>
<li> Wash
<li> Rinse
<list>
<li> Warm water
<li> Cold water
</list>
<li> Repeat
</list>
</code>

<minipage #mp:list-ordered>
<list ordered>
<li> Wash
<li> Rinse
<list>
<li> Warm water
<li> Cold water
</list>
<li> Repeat
</list>
</minipage>

<sub-section | Arguments>

<table csv -numbered>
Ordered (render as a numbered list), ordered
marker (the CSS list-style-type), decimal | lower-roman | upper-alpha | disc | …
"start (first number, ordered only)", start=3
"reversed (count down, ordered only)", reversed
Id, #list:steps
Class, .compact
</table>

<sub-section | Notes>

Items take no pipe and no closing bracket — content simply follows the marker.

Markers are recognised only at the start of a line, so an inline `<-` in prose (R's `x <- y`) is never mistaken for an item.

`marker`, `start`, and `reversed` apply to ordered lists. The markdown `-`/`*` register is the lossy one and is switched off in strict mode.
</section>

<section #sec:block-quotations | Block Quotations>

<sub-section | General Usage>

A block quotation sets a passage off from the surrounding text. Write it canonically with `<quote>`, or with a markdown `>` at the start of each line:

Canonical: `<quote {args} | Quotation text>`
Markdown: `> Quotation text`

<code #code:quote-basic>
<quote cite=https://example.com/keynes |
The difficulty lies not so much in developing
new ideas as in escaping from old ones.
>
</code>

<minipage #mp:quote-basic>
<quote cite=https://example.com/keynes |
The difficulty lies not so much in developing
new ideas as in escaping from old ones.
>
</minipage>

<sub-section | Arguments>

<table csv -numbered>
cite (URL of the source), cite=https://example.com/source
Id, #quote:keynes
Class, .pullquote
</table>
</section>

<section #sec:definition-lists | Definition Lists>

<sub-section | General Usage>

A definition list pairs terms with their definitions. `<dl>` wraps the list; each pair is a `<dt>` term and a `<dd>` definition.

<code #code:dl-basic>
<dl>
<dt | Eigenvalue>
<dd | A scalar that stretches its eigenvector under a transformation.>
<dt | Eigenvector>
<dd | A non-zero vector whose direction the transformation preserves.>
</dl>
</code>

<minipage #mp:dl-basic>
<dl>
<dt | Eigenvalue>
<dd | A scalar that stretches its eigenvector under a transformation.>
<dt | Eigenvector>
<dd | A non-zero vector whose direction the transformation preserves.>
</dl>
</minipage>

<sub-section | Arguments>

<table csv -numbered>
Id, #dl:linear-algebra
Class, .compact
</table>
</section>

<section #sec:callouts | Callouts>

<sub-section | General Usage>

A callout lifts a note out of the flow into a bordered box. Write `<aside>` and pick a flavour with `type`:

<code #code:aside-basic>
<aside type=warning |
Back up your data before running this command —
it cannot be undone.
>
</code>

<minipage #mp:aside-basic>
<aside type=warning |
Back up your data before running this command —
it cannot be undone.
>
</minipage>

<sub-section | Arguments>

<table csv -numbered>
type (the callout flavour), note | sidebar | callout | warning | tip | info | caution
Numbered (count this aside in the float sequence), +numbered
No border (drop the visual box), -border
Id, #aside:backup
Class, .my-class
</table>

<sub-section | Notes>

The emphasis flavours (callout, warning, tip, info, caution, note) draw a box around the content; `sidebar` instead pushes it into the margin.
</section>

<section #sec:collapsible-details | Collapsible Details>

<sub-section | General Usage>

A disclosure widget hides its body until the reader opens it. Wrap the body in `<details>` … `</details>` and label it with a `<summary>`:

<code #code:details-basic>
<details>
<summary | Show the proof>
By induction on n. The base case holds since…
and the inductive step follows directly.
</details>
</code>

<minipage #mp:details-basic>
<details>
<summary | Show the proof>
By induction on n. The base case holds since…
and the inductive step follows directly.
</details>
</minipage>
</section>

<section #sec:thematic-breaks | Thematic Breaks>

<sub-section | General Usage>

A thematic break is a scene-change rule between blocks. Write `---` on its own line, or the canonical self-closing `<hr/>`:

Canonical: `<hr/>`
Markdown: `---`

<code #code:hr-basic>
The first movement closes here.

---

A new movement begins.
</code>

<minipage #mp:hr-basic>
The first movement closes here.

---

A new movement begins.
</minipage>

<sub-section | Arguments>

<table csv -numbered>
type (a styling hint), type=ornamental
</table>

<sub-section | Notes>

With a `type`, use the self-closing form with a space before the slash: `<hr type=ornamental />`.
</section>
